      "id": 1,
      "text": "Jefferson City began as Mossy Creek in 1788 when settlers Adam and Elizabeth Peck built a fort, mill, and church along the Holston River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://jeffersoncitytn.gov/government/history.php",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The original name Mossy Creek came from the vivid green moss that grew around the creek near the early settlement.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jefferson_City%2C_Tennessee",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "John Roper Branner rerouted the East Tennessee Railroad in 1858, moving the line to Mountcastle Street and giving three acres for the station.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6,
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://jeffersoncitytn.gov/government/history.php",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 1940 the TVA bought land in Jefferson City to build Cherokee Dam, creating Cherokee Lake that now draws anglers and boaters.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jefferson_City%2C_Tennessee",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The late 1900s saw Jefferson City industrialize when geologists found rich zinc ore, sparking a mining boom.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25
